# G026 — News Ticker and Game Scores

*v2. See `news-ticker.md` for prose: priority + time as two-axis
sort, TTL as temporal scope managing attention, interrupts as
reverse `<-` gates forcing output, content/presentation split
applied to a stream.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Attention Ticker

[vault-ticker
    IN ORDER TO
        funnel all inbound events (alerts, scores, news) into one stream
        rendered sorted by priority then recency
        with TTL automatically pruning stale items every tick

    @ticker <- [ticker[new]]

    sync @event-sources {
        alert @text @priority ->
            [ticker[post {ticker: @ticker, text: @text,
                           category: "alert",
                           priority: @priority, ttl-seconds: 300}]]

        news @text ->
            [ticker[post {ticker: @ticker, text: @text,
                           category: "news",
                           priority: 2, ttl-seconds: 1800}]]

        score @text ->
            [ticker[post {ticker: @ticker, text: @text,
                           category: "sports",
                           priority: 1, ttl-seconds: 3600}]]
    }

    every 1 second
        [ticker[evict-expired {ticker: @ticker, now: [clock[now]]}]]
        @visible <- [ticker[snapshot {ticker: @ticker}]]
        [ui[render-scrolling {items: @visible}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[ticker
    new
    post                 {ticker, text, category, priority, ttl-seconds?}
    snapshot             {ticker}                   ;; sorted, non-expired
    evict-expired        {ticker, now}
    breaking             {ticker, text}             ;; priority 5, interrupt
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: two-axis sort `where` priority first, recency second

[ticker[snapshot {ticker: @t}]
    @t:items
        where not-expired (now < expires-at or expires-at is none)
        ordered by
            priority descending,      ;; highest priority first
            timestamp descending       ;; most recent within priority
    IN ORDER TO
        let importance dominate order while recency breaks ties
        so the agent triaging the ticker sees what matters most first
        and the freshest-of-equal-importance second
]

## Distinctive Innate idiom: TTL as temporal scope `where` now passes expires-at

[ticker[evict-expired {ticker: @t, now: @n}]
    @t:items
        where expires-at is not none
        and   expires-at is at most @n
            -> remove from @t:items
    IN ORDER TO
        prune items the caller already declared temporary
        because attention is bounded and permanent-everything is noise
]

## Distinctive Innate idiom: breaking news as interrupt `where` priority = max

[ticker[breaking {ticker: @t, text: @s}]
    post with priority 5 (max) and no TTL
    IN ORDER TO
        preempt normal flow — the `<-` gate operating in reverse,
        forcing output instead of filtering input
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @t <- [ticker[new]]
    [ticker[post {ticker: @t, text: "Mercury Retrograde",
                   category: "news", priority: 2,
                   ttl-seconds: 3600}]]
    [ticker[post {ticker: @t, text: "ETH +5%",
                   category: "finance", priority: 3,
                   ttl-seconds: 1800}]]
    [ticker[breaking {ticker: @t, text: "Security incident"}]]

    -> [ticker[snapshot {ticker: @t}]]
    ;; -> [Security incident (p5), ETH +5% (p3), Mercury Retrograde (p2)]
]
